# Line-ending policy: LF in the index and in the working tree, everywhere.
#
# This is not cosmetic. Mixed endings in this repository have produced false
# readings in contract work more than once: a concealed-record count that read 0
# against a CRLF ledger while the identical pattern read 5 against an LF one,
# and a diagnosis of test_progression_audit_contract sent down two blind alleys
# because `git checkout` and `git worktree` silently rewrote endings and broke
# different assertions than the real defect. Multi-line anchors are load-bearing
# in tools/, so the bytes under them have to be predictable.
*               text=auto eol=lf
*.pdf           -text

# --- On checkpoints, deliberately no exception ------------------------------
#
# Checkpoints are byte-frozen once promoted (Rules 13.2), and every profile
# migration text here promises they stay byte-unchanged, so the obvious instinct
# is to exclude campaigns/*/saves/** from renormalization. Measured before
# writing this rule: `git ls-files --eol` reports i/lf for all 500 files under
# saves/ and .saves/. They have always been stored as LF; the CRLF seen in the
# working tree was produced by core.autocrlf at checkout and was never committed
# canon. Applying eol=lf to them therefore changes nothing -- verified both
# ways, with and without an exclusion, and `git status` reports zero modified
# files under saves/ in each case.
#
# So an exclusion would buy no protection that the index does not already give,
# while marking those paths -text would cost readable diffs on precisely the
# files where a diff matters most: the ones that are never supposed to change,
# where an accidental edit needs to be legible at review. One uniform rule is
# both safer to reason about and better when something goes wrong.
